  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure will continue its trek eastward across southern New \nEngland with steady rain along the coastal plain coming to an end \nthis evening. Latest surface analysis shows a cold front \nthrough the St Lawrence Valley with showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping along the front. These showers and storms will \napproach the Canadian Border and northern CT Valley after sunset\nwhile CAMs suggest upwards of 500 J/kg of MU CAPE will linger \nuntil around 10 PM. This instability will bring the potential \nfor a few rumbles of thunder across the north early tonight. \n\nAs the low exits, plenty of low level moisture will remain over\nthe area before the cold front pushes offshore Monday morning.\nThis will allow for fog formation overnight with locally dense\nfog possible. As the front crosses NW winds will increase that\nwill help mix out the fog during the pre dawn hours from NW to\nSE. These NW winds may not reach the coast until after day break \nthat could lead to fog impacting the morning commute along the\ncoastal plain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface high pressure will arrive from the west on Monday before\ncresting over New England Monday night into Tuesday. Gusty\nnorthwesterly winds are expected with afternoon mixing on \nMonday with a few gusts up to around 35 mph. Skies will be \nmainly sunny south of the mountains with more in the way of \ncloud cover across the mountains and towards the Canadian border\nwith the upslope flow. High temperatures will be into the 60s \nto lower 70s, warmest along the coast and in southern NH. \n\nWinds will diminish Monday evening and remain light through the\nnight, which combined with clear skies will allow for strong\nradiational cooling. As a result, used mostly MOS guidance for\nforecast lows... which range from the lower to middle 30s in\nsome interior valleys with upper 30s/lower 40s elsewhere. Frost\nadvisories may be needed for some northern zones. \n\nIt will remain dry Tuesday into Wednesday as high pressure\nremains near the region. High temperatures on Tuesday will be\nmostly into the lower 70s before warming into the middle to\nupper 70s on Wednesday. \n\nOur next chance for showers will be on Thursday into Friday as a\nseries of weak frontal boundaries cross the region. Temperatures\nwill generally remain near seasonable averages.",
